<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/monetary-policy-central-banks">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Central banks steer the economy with <strong>interest rates</strong>. Raise rates: borrowing
    costs climb, spending cools, inflation eases. Cut rates: credit flows, spending rises,
    employment recovers. Their mandate in most rich countries: <strong>price stability (~2%
    inflation) plus maximum employment</strong> — and their main tools are the policy rate,
    balance-sheet operations (quantitative easing), and the credibility of their word.
    The steering wheel is not directly connected to the wheels: rates move an economy
    through a chain of financial decisions spread over a year or more, which is why
    central banking is equal parts mechanics and theatre — the mechanics of the
    transmission chain, and the management of what 330 million people <em>expect</em> to
    happen to prices. This page covers what central banks are and why they are
    independent, how the policy rate travels through the economy, the inflation-targeting
    framework and the Taylor rule, quantitative easing and its unwinding, expectations
    as half the fight, and the trade-offs that no setting escapes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Taylor rule — the famous recipe mapping the economy's temperature into an interest rate" widget="sliderLab" config={moneyCfg} />

  <p>
    The Taylor rule (1993): set the rate near 2% (neutral) plus inflation, tightened by 0.5
    points for each point of overheating. It describes what central banks roughly do — and gives
    markets a benchmark to be surprised by. When inflation ran to 9% in 2022, the rule said
    raise fast; the Fed did.
  </p>

  <h2>What a central bank is — and why it is independent</h2>
  <p>
    A central bank is the economy's bank: it issues the currency, holds banks'
    reserves, lends to banks in emergencies (<strong>lender of last resort</strong> —
    the 19th-century Bagehot doctrine: lend freely, at a penalty rate, against
    good collateral, in a panic), and sets the short-term interest rate at which
    the banking system borrows from it. Its political design is as important
    as its mechanics: <strong>independence</strong> — governments set the target;
    unelected technocrats choose the path to it — exists because the temptation
    to boost the economy before elections produces inflation (the 1970s
    provided the natural experiment: politically dominated banks everywhere,
    inflation everywhere). Time and again, countries that granted independence
    (New Zealand pioneered inflation targeting in 1989–90, the Bank of England
    gained operational independence in 1997) saw inflation expectations fall
    and stay lower. The mandate split matters: the Fed has a <strong>dual
    mandate</strong> (price stability and maximum employment), the ECB a
    hierarchical one (price stability first), the Bank of England a 2% CPI
    target with a letter-writing duty when inflation strays more than a
    point — institutional details that shape every rate decision.
  </p>

  <h2>The transmission chain: how a rate decision moves a continent</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>policy rate</strong> (Fed funds rate, Bank Rate, the deposit rate) is
    overnight money between banks — and everything else keys off it. The
    cascade: the policy rate sets interbank lending rates; those set banks'
    funding costs; funding costs set mortgage rates, business loan rates,
    credit card rates, and the return on savings. Higher rates then work
    through several channels at once: <strong>borrowing costs</strong> (mortgages
    and investment plans are postponed), <strong>wealth effects</strong> (higher
    discount rates lower asset prices — equities, houses — making holders
    feel poorer and spend less), <strong>exchange rates</strong> (higher rates
    attract capital, strengthening the currency, cheapening imports and
    squeezing exporters), and <strong>credit supply</strong> (banks tighten
    lending standards). The whole chain takes <strong>roughly 12–18 months</strong>
    to reach inflation — Friedman's "long and variable lags" — which
    creates the central banker's permanent dilemma: they must act on
    forecasts, defend actions whose effects are not visible for years,
    and accept that by the time the evidence arrives, conditions may
    have changed. The 2022–2023 cycle sharpened the point: rates went
    from near zero to over 5% in about 18 months — the fastest
    tightening since the 1980s — with the full effects still landing
    years later.
  </p>

  <h2>Inflation targeting and the Taylor rule</h2>
  <p>
    The modern framework is <strong>inflation targeting</strong>: announce a target
    (2% nearly everywhere — a number with folkloric origins in New Zealand's
    1989 design, now entrenched by inertia), explain deviations publicly,
    and move rates according to a reaction function. The <strong>Taylor rule</strong>
    (John Taylor, 1993) is the famous recipe: policy rate ≈ neutral real rate
    (about 2%) + current inflation + 0.5 × (inflation − target) + 0.5 ×
    (output gap) — tighten when inflation runs hot <em>and</em> when the economy
    runs above capacity. <strong>A worked reading:</strong> with inflation at 2%,
    target 2%, and a zero output gap, the rule says about 4% nominal — the
    "neutral" setting. With 9% inflation and a +2% gap (2022), it says
    2 + 9 + 0.5×7 + 1 = over 15% — describing the aggressive tightening
    that followed (actual rates peaked lower, but the direction matched).
    No bank follows the rule mechanically — judgement, financial stability
    and the zero floor intervene — but it disciplines debate: it gives
    markets a benchmark to be surprised by, and makes deviations
    explainable ("we see a larger output gap than the rule assumes").
    The deeper economics underneath is the <strong>Phillips curve</strong>: inflation
    responds to the output gap (the unemployment lesson's trade-off), so
    a central bank buying lower inflation is buying it with slower growth
    — the exchange rate of the trade is the steepest political question
    in macroeconomics.
  </p>

  <h2>Quantitative easing: the balance sheet as a tool</h2>
  <p>
    When the policy rate hit the <strong>zero lower bound</strong> (2008, 2020),
    conventional policy was exhausted — so central banks reached for the
    balance sheet. <strong>Quantitative easing (QE)</strong>: the central bank
    creates bank reserves (its own liabilities) and uses them to buy
    long-term government bonds (and, at the Fed, mortgage-backed
    securities) from financial institutions. The Fed's balance sheet
    grew from about <strong>$900 billion before 2008 to roughly $9 trillion
    by 2022</strong>. The mechanism is a portfolio swap: sellers rebalance
    into other assets, pushing down long-term yields across the economy,
    while the sheer commitment signals that rates stay low for long —
    QE as rate policy by other means. The common misreading — "they
    printed money and gave it away" — misses that the money is bank
    reserves, held inside the banking system and paying interest, not
    household cash. The 2020s added the reverse gear,
    <strong>quantitative tightening (QT)</strong>: letting bonds mature (and,
    at some banks, actively selling), shrinking balance sheets —
    monetary policy in reverse, an experiment whose full effects are
    still being measured. Negative policy rates (ECB, Japan, mid-2010s)
    pushed the same frontier further: charging banks to hold reserves,
    with murky evidence and clear limits (cash in vaults is the floor).
  </p>

  <h2>Expectations: half the fight happens in heads</h2>
  <p>
    Wages are set on what workers expect prices to do; prices are set on
    what firms expect wages to do; and the loop closes on itself — which
    is why <strong>expectations</strong> determine how costly disinflation is. If
    the public believes the bank will hit 2%, contracts embed 2%, and
    inflation drifts there without mass unemployment. If credibility
    breaks — as in the 1970s, when two oil shocks and political
    pressure broke the Fed's word — expectations un-anchor, and
    inflation can only be forced out through recession: Arthur
    Burns's accommodative 1970s Fed versus <strong>Paul Volcker's</strong>
    1980–82 squeeze (rates near <strong>20%</strong>, two recessions,
    unemployment near 11%, inflation down from 14% to under 4%) is
    the reference case of credibility's price. The modern toolkit
    manages expectations as deliberately as it manages rates:
    <strong>forward guidance</strong> ("rates will stay low until unemployment
    falls below 6%"), inflation reports, dot plots, press conferences —
    "talk is policy", because the cheapest rate decision is the one
    the market makes on your behalf.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the central bank sets all interest rates."
  It sets one overnight rate; every other rate — mortgages, corporate
  bonds, credit cards — is a market price built from expectations of the
  future path of that rate plus risk premia. This is why rate
  <em>announcements</em> can move long-term rates without any action, and why
  markets can fight the bank: if investors expect more inflation, they
  demand higher long-term yields whatever the overnight rate is —
  the bond market's daily audit of the central bank's credibility.</p>

  <h2>The trade-offs that no setting escapes</h2>
  <p>
    Monetary policy has no free moves. <strong>Fighting inflation costs
    jobs:</strong> the Phillips curve converts tighter policy into slower growth
    and higher unemployment — the Volcker arithmetic. <strong>Fighting
    recessions risks finance:</strong> a decade of near-zero rates inflated
    asset prices (housing, equities), rewarded leverage, and — the
    "zombification" critique — kept unproductive firms alive. <strong>The
    distribution is uneven:</strong> easing raises asset prices (owners gain)
    while tightening raises unemployment (workers pay) — so monetary
    policy redistributes by class, whatever its neutral intent.
    <strong>Exchange rates bind smaller economies:</strong> a small open
    economy's rates move its currency, importing or exporting other
    countries' shocks. The institutional answer to all of these is not a
    better dial but transparency about the dial's cost — which is why
    the inflation-targeting era made its trade-offs explicit in every
    forecast round.
  </p>

  <h2>Lender of last resort: Bagehot's three rules</h2>
  <p>
    The central bank's oldest tool is not a dial but a promise, and Walter
    Bagehot wrote its manual during the 1866 Overend Gurney panic:
    in a crisis, the central bank should <strong>lend freely</strong> (stop the
    contagion), <strong>at a penalty rate</strong> (so banks use it only when
    genuinely illiquid, not for cheap funding), <strong>against good
    collateral</strong> (so the lender of last resort funds solvent-but-
    illiquid banks, not insolvent ones). The three clauses are the
    whole dilemma: lend without the penalty and you subsidise risk;
    lend without collateral and you rescue the insolvent; refuse to
    lend at all and a solvent system dies of a queue. Every financial
    rescue since is a variation: 2008's facilities were Bagehot with
    wider collateral lists; 2023's bank failures tested the same
    clauses with faster depositors (mobile banking moving billions
    in hours). The tool sits oddly beside inflation targeting —
    crisis lending expands the balance sheet exactly when the
    dial says stand down — which is why the two functions are
    run as separate rooms of the same institution, and why
    the collateral rules, not the headline amounts, are
    where the accountability lives.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The 2021–2024 stress test.</strong> "Transitory" was the mistake heard
    round the world: supply shocks, stimulus and shifting demand met a
    framework built for demand shortfalls. The inflation peaked at 9% in
    the UK, 9.1% in the US (June 2022), and the subsequent tightening
    brought prices down without (through 2024) the deep recession the
    Phillips curve predicted — reopening the curve-steepness debate
    (anchored expectations did more work than joblessness). The
    framework reviews now underway are rewriting the lessons in
    real time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier.</strong> Central bank digital currencies (CBDCs) would
    give the public direct central-bank money — a run-risk question, a
    privacy question and a monetary-policy question at once. Climate
    mandates are pulling banks into credit allocation (contested
    territory for institutions built on market neutrality). And the
    independence consensus itself is under political pressure in
    several countries — the 1970s lesson is one generation old, and
    institutional memory decays.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Taylor (1993); Friedman, "The Role of Monetary Policy" (1968); Bagehot, Lombard Street (1873); Bernanke, The Federal Reserve and the Financial Crisis; the unemployment lesson's Phillips-curve material.</p>

